# Kalantar et al. (2025), JGR Solid Earth, 10.1029/2025JB031938.
# Specimen OG-T -- TENSILE fracture in Odenwald granodiorite.
#
# Units: m.
# Geometry (paper Table 1): length = 0.10000, diameter = 0.04998,
# fracture angle = 28 degrees, measured from the specimen's long/vertical axis to
# the fracture plane.
#
# ============================================================================
# READ THIS BEFORE USING THE MESH: TABLE 1 AND TABLE 2 DISAGREE BY 2.00 DEGREES
# ============================================================================
# The paper's own equations (3), (4) and (5) give an identity in which every term
# is tabulated:
#
#     tan(theta) = (sigma'_n - sigma_3 + P_p) / tau,     P_p = (P_i + 3)/2 MPa
#
# Regressing it over Table 2's stages returns, per specimen:
#
#     OG-SH   printed 29.0   recovered 29.006   sigma_3 = 32.996   max resid 0.004 MPa
#     OG-SC   printed 30.0   recovered 29.998   sigma_3 = 33.002   max resid 0.006 MPa
#     OG-T    printed 28.0   recovered 25.999   sigma_3 = 33.001   max resid 0.007 MPa
#
# Two of the three reproduce their printed angle to 0.006 degrees, so the method is
# sound and it is OG-T that is anomalous. The recovery is not a loose fit either:
# a residual of 0.007 MPa across seventeen stages whose tau spans 19.4 to 63.2 MPa
# means two constants regenerate the entire stress table. Forcing theta = 28 leaves
# a systematic residual of (tan28 - tan26)*tau = 0.85 to 2.78 MPa, which no
# constant sigma_3 and no constant P_o can absorb -- both were tested.
#
# WHICH ONE IS WRONG, AND WHY THIS JOURNAL USES 28
# ------------------------------------------------
# Geometry settles it. A through-going elliptical fracture at angle theta needs an
# axial extent of 2r*cot(theta):
#
#     at 28 deg   93.999 mm   fits the 100.00 mm core, clearing each end by 3.00 mm
#     at 26 deg  102.474 mm   DOES NOT FIT -- it overruns each end face by 1.24 mm
#
# So 26 degrees is impossible for a specimen that is 100.00 mm long and 49.98 mm in
# diameter, and Table 1 is internally consistent while Table 2's reduction is not.
# The reading is that OG-T's stress columns were reduced with the wrong angle.
# 28 degrees is therefore the PHYSICAL angle and is what this journal cuts.
#
# WHAT THAT COSTS WHEN COMPARING WITH TABLE 2
# -------------------------------------------
# The published sigma'_n and tau columns for OG-T are in a 26-degree frame, so they
# cannot be compared directly with a 28-degree model. Recover the differential
# stress the experiment actually measured and re-resolve it:
#
#     sigma_d      = tau_published / (sin26 cos26)   = tau_published / 0.393996
#     tau_28       = sigma_d sin28 cos28             = tau_published * 1.052088
#     sigma'_n,28  = (sigma_3 - P_p) + sigma_d sin^2(28)
#                  = (33 - P_p) + tau_published * 0.559407
#
# scripts/kalantar_parameter_audit.py section 6 prints the re-reduced OG-T table.
# Score against THAT, not against the printed columns. Note the size of it: the
# peak shear stress moves from 63.21 to 66.50 MPa, and the fitted strength
# criterion tau = 1.1 sigma'_n was itself fitted in the 26-degree frame, so it must
# be re-fitted before it is used to set the critical stress state.
#
# The alternative -- meshing at 26 degrees -- is kept in
# kalantar2025_og_t_theta26.jou, but it cannot be built without contradicting one of
# Table 1's measured dimensions. Use it only as a sensitivity arm.
#
# Plane, centred at z = L/2 = 0.050, at x = +/- r = 0.02499:
#     z = L/2 +/- r cot(28 deg) = 0.050 +/- 0.046999354
#     z_high = 0.096999354      z_low = 0.003000646
#
# Expected fracture_interface area after the split: pi r^2 / sin(28 deg)
#     = 4.179007e-3 m^2.
#
# THE TIP CLEARANCE IS ONLY 3.00 mm. That is smaller than one factor-5 element, so
# the fracture tip will sit within a single element of the end face and the loading
# platen. Check the meshed result at both tips before trusting a stress
# concentration there, and expect this specimen to want the factor-3 mesh for
# production rather than as a convergence check.
#
# AFTER REGENERATING, pin the source coordinates onto real interface nodes.
# Boreholes are 2 mm across, 5 mm from the sidewall, one at each end (section 2.3),
# so the intended points are x = +/- (r - 0.005) = +/- 0.019990:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.019990 0 0.012404278'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.019990 0 0.087595722'
# use_closest_node = true snaps silently, including onto bulk nodes off the
# fracture. Run scripts/check_source_nodes.py and write the exact node coordinates
# back into the deck. The 5 mm inset is ambiguous between hole centre and hole edge;
# see the note in the OG-SH journal.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-23 against kalantar2025_og_t_theta28_size3.e (53,760 elements,
# 2297 interface nodes, median interface pitch 0.980 mm). Both sources land ON the
# fracture, 792.9 um from the design point. USE THESE IN THE DECK:
#     source_in   coord = '-0.020362222 0 0.011704230'
#     source_out  coord = ' 0.020362222 0 0.088295770'
# Modelled borehole sits 4.628 mm from the sidewall (design 5.000); borehole
# separation 86.7453 mm, +1.862 %, which is a +0.421 % bias on Q through the
# ln(2*l/T - 1) factor of eq (7). Note this displacement is SMALLER than the
# hole-centre/hole-edge ambiguity in the paper's own 5 mm statement (1.000 mm), so
# it is not the dominant uncertainty on the flow path -- but the deck must still
# carry the snapped separation, not the design one.
#
# The mesh also confirms the tip-clearance warning above: L and theta come out
# exactly as designed (100.00 mm, 28.000 deg, plane-fit residual 0.00 um) and the
# fracture_interface area is 4.179007e-3 m^2, matching the derivation to 6 digits.
#
# MESH SIZE. "auto factor" is a Cubit coarseness index, not a length. Factor 5 is
# the production discretisation; factor 3 is the convergence sibling.

reset
create Cylinder height 0.10000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.050 include_merged

create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.096999354
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.096999354
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.003000646
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge

# ==============================================================================
# BOREHOLE VERTEX IMPRINT -- a mesh node at each source BY CONSTRUCTION.
# Added 2026-08-24. NOT YET RUN IN CUBIT -- see "IF THE SPLIT FAILS" below.
# ==============================================================================
# WHY. Both boreholes sit at y = 0 on the fracture plane. That is not the interior
# of a surface: it is exactly the line where the yplane webcut above cut the
# fracture ellipse -- the ellipse's MAJOR AXIS -- and it is a geometric curve.
# Cubit divides that curve into N EQUAL intervals (measured: min spacing == max
# spacing to machine precision), so the only node positions the source can reach
# are k/N along it. The design borehole sits at x/r = 0.01999/0.02499 = 0.799920,
# so the pinning error is integer arithmetic, round(0.79992 N)/N - 0.79992:
#
#     mesh              N    nearest fraction     pinning error
#     _size3  OG-SH     25   20/25 = 0.800000         4.1 um   <-- N divisible by 5
#     _size3  OG-T      27   22/27 = 0.814815       792.9 um
#     _size3  OG-SC     26   21/26 = 0.807692       388.5 um
#     graded  OG-SH     28   22/28 = 0.785714       732.2 um
#     graded  OG-T      29   23/29 = 0.793103       362.8 um
#     graded  OG-SC     27   22/27 = 0.814815       744.4 um
#
# All six reproduce the independently measured check_source_nodes.py distance to
# 0.1 um. THIS SPECIMEN'S 792.9 um MISS IS NOT A MESH DEFECT -- it is 27 not being
# divisible by 5. It also explains the one puzzle in the graded experiment above:
# the graded OG-T mesh pinned BETTER (362.8 um) purely because 29 lands closer to
# 0.79992 than 27 does, nothing to do with the sizing that was being tested.
#
# Splitting the curve at the design position puts a VERTEX there, and Cubit meshes
# every vertex with a node. Pinning then becomes EXACT and INDEPENDENT of mesh
# size, which is the thing that lets this mesh be coarsened at all.
#
# The split adds one vertex and replaces one curve with two. It creates no new
# surfaces and no new volumes, so the hardcoded block/nodeset surface IDs and the
# nodeset 5/6 vertex IDs further down are unaffected. Do NOT try to do this with a
# webcut instead -- that renumbers everything.
#
# THIS SPECIMEN GAINS THE MOST FROM IT. The 792.9 um miss puts the modelled
# borehole 4.628 mm from the sidewall against a 5.000 mm design and stretches the
# separation to 86.7453 mm, +1.862 % on a SCORED channel. The imprint takes that
# to the design 85.1596 mm exactly, which is also the paper's value.
#
# CURVE IDS: run Examples/Kalantar2025/mesh_probe_axis_curves.jou once and paste
# them here. They are the two curves of length 53.2301 mm (= r/sin28) lying in
# surfaces 26 46 53 32; that length is unique in the model. c_pos runs to +x
# (towards z_high = 0.096999354), c_neg to -x (towards z_low = 0.003000646).
# Left at 0 deliberately, so an unfilled journal fails loudly on "curve 0".
#{ c_pos = 0 }
#{ c_neg = 0 }
split curve {c_pos} location position  0.019990 0 0.087595722
split curve {c_neg} location position -0.019990 0 0.012404278
#
# IF THE SPLIT FAILS -- if Cubit rejects `split curve` on merged geometry, or the
# mesh quality drops near the new vertex -- comment the two split lines out and
# uncomment the two below instead. Forcing N divisible by 5 puts a node at exactly
# 0.8 r = 0.019992, i.e. 4.3 um from design and a separation of 85.1682 mm
# (+0.010 %), at ANY global coarseness, with no change to the topology at all.
#   curve {c_pos} {c_neg} interval 25
#   curve {c_pos} {c_neg} scheme equal
# ==============================================================================

imprint all
merge all

# ==============================================================================
# GRADED SIZING: TRIED 2026-08-24 AND MEASURED. REJECTED FOR THE CAMPAIGN -- but on
# THIS specimen it came out better; see the note under "WHAT IT COST". Read before
# trying it again. The premise was wrong, not the execution.
# ==============================================================================
# The idea was to size the fracture and the bulk independently -- fracture 0.95 mm,
# bulk 1.70 mm -- on the theory that these meshes were over-refined in the BULK and a
# large fraction of the elements sat in rock where nothing happens.
#
# THEY WERE NOT. Median node spacing [mm] against distance from the fracture plane,
# measured on the exported meshes:
#
#     distance      0-1   1-2   2-4   4-8  8-16  16-32  32-100 mm
#     _size3.e     0.957 0.939 0.969 0.944 0.895 0.885  0.926
#     graded       0.905 0.941 0.903 0.882 0.832 0.824  0.862
#
# Both profiles are FLAT. The mesh was already uniform at ~0.93 mm everywhere out to
# 100 mm from the fracture; there was no over-refined bulk to recover. And the
# `volume all size` line had no effect at all -- the SURFACE size propagated through
# the entire volume and made the whole mesh finer.
#
# The reason is the element type. `scheme polyhedron` produces HEX8 here, and a hex
# mesher propagates surface intervals along the mapped directions through the whole
# volume. It cannot grade away from an interior surface the way a tet mesher can.
# Refining the fracture necessarily refines everything.
#
# WHAT IT COST ON THIS SPECIMEN, measured:
#     elements        53,760 ->  51,600   (-4.0 %)
#     interface nodes  2,297 ->   2,641   (+15.0 %)
#     interface pitch  0.980 ->   0.916 mm
#     source pinning   PASSES  ->  PASSES, and IMPROVES: the boreholes land 362.8 um
#                                  from design against 792.9 um on _size3.e -- the
#                                  only mesh of the three where the graded version
#                                  pins BETTER
#     re-pinned separation 86.7453 -> 84.4339 mm (paper value 85.1596), so the graded
#     mesh is also CLOSER to the paper's flow path: -0.85 % against +1.86 %.
#     mesh_flow_width_over_length_og_t would move +2.74 %, on a SCORED channel.
#
# SO: on OG-T alone the graded mesh is strictly better on every axis except that it
# saves no meaningful time. It is NOT adopted now, because OG-T's open problem is a
# preload defect (see the WARNING in 110_04_og_t_bbfast_r3.i) and changing the mesh
# underneath an unresolved model defect makes the diagnosis unattributable. If OG-T
# needs a rebuild AFTER the preload probe closes, use the graded pair below and
# re-derive mesh_flow_width_over_length_og_t from the new separation.
#
# *** AMENDED 2026-08-24, later the same day. DO NOT ACT ON THE PINNING CLAIM. ***
# The 792.9 -> 362.8 um "improvement" above is not a property of the graded sizing.
# It is 29 landing closer to 0.79992 than 27 does -- see the BOREHOLE VERTEX IMPRINT
# block below, where all six meshes are predicted from the interval count alone. The
# imprint gets 0 um at ANY sizing, so there is no longer any pinning reason to prefer
# the graded pair. What survives of the paragraph above is only the -4.0 % element
# count and the finer pitch, neither of which is worth a rebuild on its own.
#
# HOW THE WRONG PREMISE WAS REACHED, so it is not repeated: the "implied bulk edge"
# was inferred as (volume/n_elements)^(1/3) and compared against the interface pitch,
# and the ratio was read as weak grading. Those two numbers are not commensurable --
# one is a 3D element-volume scale, the other a 2D in-plane node spacing -- so the
# ratio was an artefact of the comparison, not a property of the mesh. The
# spacing-versus-distance profile above is the direct measurement, it costs one
# command, and it should have been run first.
#
# IF THIS MESH EVER NEEDS TO BE CHEAPER: it cannot be done by grading with this
# scheme, only by coarsening globally. The lever is not the mesh -- use 128 ranks.
# The durable fix would be to imprint the two borehole vertices into the geometry so
# a node exists at each source BY CONSTRUCTION, making source pinning exact and
# mesh-independent and allowing free coarsening. Not attempted.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACTIVE SIZING: the production mesh, uniform, factor 3. See the OG-T note above.
vol all size auto factor 3  # 5 -> kalantar2025_og_t_theta28_size5.e ; 3 -> _size3.e
volume all scheme polyhedron
mesh volume all

# The graded pair, kept so the measurement above can be reproduced. If you uncomment
# these, change the export name at the bottom to _size3.e:
#   volume all size 0.00170
#   surface 26 46 53 32 size 0.00095

list totals   # element count

block 1 add volume 2 4 8 6
block 1 name "top_block"

block 2 add volume 1 3 5 7
block 2 name "bottom_block"

# Surface and vertex IDs follow this exact construction order and are shared by all
# four Ye2018 journals. Confirm "fracture_interface" reports the area quoted in the
# header before exporting -- a mis-numbered nodeset fails silently, not loudly.
nodeset 1 add surface 36 56 63 42
nodeset 1 name "top_nodeset"
nodeset 2 add surface 28 30 50 48
nodeset 2 name "bottom_nodeset"
nodeset 3 add surface 33 43 61 51 25 35 57 47
nodeset 3 name "sides_nodeset"
nodeset 4 add surface 26 46 53 32
nodeset 4 name "fracture_interface"
nodeset 5 add vertex 27 34 41 48
nodeset 5 name "no_disp_x"
nodeset 6 add vertex 10 11 18 19
nodeset 6 name "no_disp_y"

# VERIFY BEFORE EXPORTING. Element and interface-node counts will now differ from
# the pre-imprint mesh (53,760 / 2297) because the split adds a constrained vertex;
# the fracture AREA must not move -- it is the same geometry.
list nodeset 4                          # interface nodes
list surface 26 46 53 32 geometry       # fracture area, expect 4.179007e-3 m^2

# EXPORTED UNDER A NEW NAME ON PURPOSE. _size3.e is the mesh 110_04 points at; do
# not overwrite it with an untested mesh. Promote _pin.e to production only after
# BOTH of these pass, from the repository root in the `moose` conda environment:
#     python3 scripts/check_axis_intervals.py <this mesh>   -> expect 0.0 um
#     python3 scripts/check_source_nodes.py  <this mesh> \
#             -0.019990 0 0.012404278   0.019990 0 0.087595722
# If they pass, the deck's source coordinates become the DESIGN values above and
# mesh_flow_width_over_length_og_t must be re-derived from the design separation
# of 85.1596 mm -- which is the paper's own value, so the +1.862 % bias goes away.
export mesh "kalantar2025_og_t_theta28_size3_pin.e" overwrite
